From 21d47c99dbe31322002d3f9cf5633801575b1a41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 22:24:01 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0206 -- MIDI timing report: lateness, bursts,
 and song clock against the RTC

WHY: the operator's listen of the 8.3 hardware captures: "the real hardware sounds way better than
dosbox, although I think the tempo might be slower and it sounds like music speeds up randomly."
midi_sched is ticked from the main loop (AudioPlayer::MidiPoll -> midi_sched_tick(now), once per
tick, uclock wall time), and midi_sched_tick dispatches everything due by `now`. So a main-loop
stall holds every note due during it, and the next tick releases the whole backlog at once -- a
pause, then a rush. Nothing measured that, and the captures could not (the intro repeats every ~2 s,
too short for a window aligner to hold a bar). "Slower overall" is a separate question: the song
clock is uclock, so it can only be slow if uclock runs slow against real time.

WHAT: after each midi_sched_tick(), MidiPoll reports the events dispatched and the scheduler-clock
gap since the previous poll to dos_midi_backend_note_tick(). The gap bounds how late any of those
events was. Kept: the largest gap that dispatched anything (late_max_ms), an events-weighted gap
histogram (<=25/50/100/250/500/>500 ms), the events dispatched in gaps over 50 ms (late50), the
largest single-poll burst and its loop tick (burst=N@tick), and song-clock ms against the CMOS RTC
read at the first and last poll (clk=<ms>/<s>s; the RTC is its own crystal, independent of the PIT
behind uclock; midi_sched_tick's 10 s gap clamp is applied the same way). A poll with a gap over
100 ms that dispatched events logs "DOS-PORT midilate tick= gap_ms= events=" for lining up with
stall ticks. All appended to the MIDI fragment of audio_vital_status. Reporting only: no scheduling
change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Nz9XeKeVG8Ab7dm8esJsMu
